# Abyss / Kalman robotics lab

Audience: curious portfolio visitors and robotics learners. Build a striking, useful interactive Kalman-filter simulation, linked as fourth project from Secret. Static HTML/CSS/JS, no backend/build/CDN requirements. Existing projects and Worker remain functional. Use kalman/ for the standalone activity. Preserve original site conventions outside it. Original root is clean; baseline 3deee5f45039401fa73f5ce21fab8a24cfd25fb6. Ancestor guidance: C:/Users/steph/Documents/AGENTS.md, copied verbatim into each worktree AGENTS.md.

Mandatory journeys: launch from Secret; understand truth vs measurement vs estimate; play/pause/step/reset a mission; change assumed Q/R separately from actual noise; compare repeatable runs; observe dropout/current/bias; understand predict/update and confidence vs actual error; use mobile and keyboard.

Implement a genuine linear Kalman filter with position/velocity and known acceleration, either one-dimensional underwater depth or independent planar axes. Use fixed time steps and seeded noise. Position fixes are simulated processed localization fixes, not raw sonar bearings/ranges or GPS underwater. DVL, if present, measures velocity. Ground truth never enters filter updates. Use valid Q consistent with disturbance model and Joseph covariance correction. Explain units, variance vs standard deviation, model limitations and any deliberately mismatched scenarios. Label RoboSub-inspired, not official course/scoring.

Features: visually compelling underwater robot scene with distinct labeled trace shapes; uncertainty band/ellipse correctly labeled; live numerical telemetry and RMSE for raw measurements/filter/dead reckoning; scenario presets (nominal, dropout, current, sensor bias); controls for actual sensor noise and assumed measurement/process noise; seed replay; teaching panel/worked predict-correct example and learner prompt; comparison of same data with changed tuning; finite mission endpoint and meaningful summary. Manual sensor toggling optional if preset dropout clearly works. All mandatory features apply equally to all designs.

Controls native/keyboard-operable, visible focus, 44px touch targets, no page overflow at 320px, no forced autoplay, honor reduced-motion, readable semantic text equivalent to plots, no per-frame screen-reader announcements. No-JS explanatory fallback. Never intercept unrelated page keys. Keep dependencies optional: distinctive custom CSS/SVG/canvas is encouraged; external UI libraries only if justified and installed locally.

Acceptance: test exact filter fixture h=1,u=0,Q=0,R=1,x=[0,0],P=I,z=3 -> x=[2,1], K=[2/3,1/3], P=[[2/3,1/3],[1/3,2/3]]. Prediction-only dropout. Replay deterministic with same seed and controls; tuning does not change measurement stream. Covariance finite, symmetric, PSD over long run. Verify meaningful fixed-seed RMSE, no requirement to always improve. Test all site regressions; update three-card test to four and footer. Syntax and diff checks. Browser verification desktop 1440x900, phone 390x844 and 320x740 where available. Record unsupported checks honestly. Preview ports clarity 8311, cinematic 8312, workbench 8313, final 8314. Node preview server or Python http.server permitted. Do not publish, push, commit blanket changes, read rival code, or alter shared run infrastructure.

Sources: https://www.cs.unc.edu/~welch/media/pdf/kalman_intro.pdf ; https://www.bzarg.com/p/how-a-kalman-filter-works-in-pictures/ ; https://robonation.gitbook.io/robosub-resources/section-3-autonomy-challenge/3.2-task-descriptions ; https://robosub.org/programs/2026/ ; https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/ . RoboSub task inspiration gate/slalom/bin navigation, competition details deliberately simplified. Research recommendations are hypotheses, not user-study evidence.
# Frozen evaluation

Mandatory gate: all PROJECT_BRIEF features and actual correct numerical filter, independent implementation, Secret integration and regression verification. Unknown checks are unknown, not passes. No fabricated user testing or benchmarks.

Weighted subjective scoring after gate: visual quality/engagement 30%, learning clarity/usability 30%, functionality/numerical correctness 20%, accessibility/responsiveness 10%, maintainability/performance 10%. Same seed 42 and nominal/dropout/current/bias tasks. Same viewport 1440x900,390x844,320x740. Compare: explain one correction; run dropout and identify growing uncertainty; replay identical sensor data at different R; reset/reach mission end; keyboard control use. Performance measured only if actual measurements exist.

Each delivery includes actual commands/results, files, preview instructions, rationale and limitations. Fresh independent reviewers inspect usability/visuals, functionality/accessibility, technical quality after candidates complete. Integration selects a coherent foundation and specific compatible improvements rather than merging everything. Final tests must be rerun.
