+  <section class="learning" aria-labelledby="learn-title"><div class="learning-intro"><p class="eyebrow">INSIDE THE ESTIMATOR</p><h2 id="learn-title">Predict. Listen.<br><em>Correct.</em></h2><p>The filter never sees the white truth trace. It combines a motion model with imperfect depth fixes, and tracks how uncertain that estimate is.</p><p class="lesson-prompt"><strong>Try this:</strong> Run dropout to 20 s. Step forward and watch the interval widen. Then save a baseline and increase R: why does the filter hesitate when fixes return?</p></div><div class="lesson-cards"><article><span class="lesson-number">01</span><div><h3>Predict the next state</h3><p>Depth advances using vertical velocity and known acceleration. Uncertain acceleration adds covariance Q.</p><code>d⁻ = d + h·v + ½h²·u<br>v⁻ = v + h·u<br>P⁻ = FPFᵀ + Q</code></div></article><article><span class="lesson-number">02</span><div><h3>Weigh the new fix</h3><p>The innovation is the fix minus predicted depth. Gain K balances predicted uncertainty against assumed sensor variance R.</p><code>y = z − d⁻ &nbsp; S = P⁻₀₀ + R<br>K = [P⁻₀₀, P⁻₁₀]ᵀ / S</code></div></article><article><span class="lesson-number">03</span><div><h3>Correct, without seeing truth</h3><p>Move depth and velocity by K·y. A missing fix skips correction; the prediction and its growing uncertainty remain.</p><code>x = x⁻ + K·y<br>P = (I−KH)P⁻(I−KH)ᵀ + KRKᵀ</code></div></article></div></section>
+  <section class="worked panel" aria-labelledby="worked-title"><div class="panel-head"><h2 id="worked-title">ONE CORRECTION, UNPACKED</h2><span id="worked-time" class="micro">STEP TO SEE LIVE NUMBERS</span></div><div id="worked-numbers" class="worked-grid"><div><span>PREDICTION</span><strong>What motion suggests</strong></div><div><span>INNOVATION</span><strong>Fix − prediction</strong></div><div><span>POSITION GAIN</span><strong>How much to trust the fix</strong></div><div><span>CORRECTION</span><strong>Prediction + gain × innovation</strong></div></div><details><summary>See a simple exact example</summary><p>Let h = 1 s, acceleration u = 0, Q = 0, R = 1 m², initial [depth, velocity] = [0, 0], and P = identity. Predicting gives P⁻ = [[2, 1], [1, 1]]. A fix z = 3 m gives gain [2/3, 1/3]. The corrected state is [2 m, 1 m/s] and P = [[2/3, 1/3], [1/3, 2/3]]. Depth moves two thirds of the way toward the fix.</p></details></section>
+  <details class="model-details panel" open><summary>Model, units & honest limits</summary><div class="model-copy"><p><strong>One axis, fixed steps.</strong> State x = [depth (m), vertical velocity (m/s)]. h = 0.2 s; F = [[1,h],[0,1]], B = [h²/2,h]ᵀ, H = [1,0]. Initial state [7,0.1], initial P = diag(1 m², 0.04 m²/s²). Known commanded acceleration is 0.07 cos(0.2t) − 0.035 sin(0.4t) m/s².</p><p><strong>Disturbance-consistent Q.</strong> Actual acceleration disturbance is independent Gaussian noise held constant per step. q is its assumed variance in (m/s²)², and Q = qBBᵀ = q[[h⁴/4,h³/2],[h³/2,h²]]. R is depth-fix variance in m². Standard deviation σ has the original units; variance is σ². The default values are rounded to four significant figures.</p><p><strong>What is a fix?</strong> A simulated, already processed localization depth fix, sampled every 0.2 s. This is not raw sonar range/bearing, underwater GPS, or DVL. No velocity sensor is modeled. Truth is used only for drawing and evaluation. Dead reckoning integrates commands with the same initial state and no fixes.</p><p><strong>Confidence has assumptions.</strong> The ribbon is the marginal depth interval d̂ ± 1.96√P₀₀, approximately 95% for a calibrated Gaussian model. It is not a 2D ellipse or a safety guarantee. Current adds an unknown acceleration of +0.045 m/s² from 20–40 s; bias adds +2 m to fixes after 20 s. Both deliberately violate the zero-mean assumptions. Dropout removes fixes from 20–36 s.</p><p><strong>Learning simulation.</strong> A prescribed depth transect, not closed-loop vehicle control, collision detection, or official RoboSub scoring. The gate, slalom, and bin references are simplified inspiration. Drag, attitude, full vehicle dynamics and correlated sensor errors are outside this model.</p><p class="sources">Further reading: <a href="https://www.cs.unc.edu/~welch/media/pdf/kalman_intro.pdf">Welch & McGee · Kalman introduction</a> · <a href="https://www.bzarg.com/p/how-a-kalman-filter-works-in-pictures/">Kalman filters in pictures</a> · <a href="https://robonation.gitbook.io/robosub-resources/section-3-autonomy-challenge/3.2-task-descriptions">RoboSub task inspiration</a> · <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/">WCAG 2.2</a></p></div></details>
